      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fruitport got its name when the Pere Marquette Railroad built a station there, turning the fertile fruit‑growing area into a port.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruitport%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Edward L. Craw founded the settlement in 1868, first calling it Crawville before it became Fruitport.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruitport%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village officially incorporated in 1891, establishing its own local government within Fruitport Charter Township.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruitport%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Fruitport sits at the upstream end of Spring Lake, which drains into Lake Michigan via the Grand River at Grand Haven.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruitport%2C_Michigan"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fruitport Charter Township blends historic roots with a vibrant community spirit, welcoming residents and visitors alike.",
